// Reading an existing value at the **open `dyn`** type argument where the declaration makes it
// unsound — refused at check time, one `E0007` per widening, exactly as the same widening to a
// `dyn Trait` is refused in the sibling file.
//
// Four widenings over three distinct occurrences of the parameter:
//
//   * `Slot` is a reference `class` with a `mut` field of the parameter's type. A widened view of a
//     class IS the original — class values have reference identity — so `wide.v = 5` stores an `int`
//     into a `Slot<Dog>`, and the original's `v.speak()` then finds an `int`.
//   * `Matcher` takes the parameter as a **method parameter**. Not aliasing at all: the body of
//     `matches` is checked believing `other` is a `Dog`, so calling it through a widened receiver
//     hands it something else. It holds for a value `struct` for the same reason, which is why
//     `Pair` is refused too.
//   * `Callback` reaches that same occurrence through a field of function type: `wide.f` would be a
//     `(dyn) -> string`, and calling it hands anything at all to a body checked at `Dog`.
//
// The last one widens to an abstract KIND rather than to `dyn`, because the rule is one rule: a
// `Slot<Struct>` view would take a store of any struct at all, which is the `Slot` case again in a
// different spelling.
//
// Every widening sits in a function **nothing calls**: the refusal is static, so no program runs at
// all, and a runtime abort could not produce these.
// expect: error E0007 at 66:23
// expect: error E0007 at 71:26
// expect: error E0007 at 76:23
// expect: error E0007 at 81:27
// expect: error E0007 at 86:26
// expect: exit 1
trait Speak {
    fn speak(): string
}

struct Dog {
    impl Speak {
        pub fn speak(): string {
            return "woof"
        }
    }
}

class Slot<T> {
    pub mut v: T
}

class Matcher<T> {
    pub v: T

    pub fn matches(other: T): bool {
        return self.v == other
    }
}

struct Pair<T> {
    v: T

    pub fn matches(other: T): bool {
        return self.v == other
    }
}

class Callback<T> {
    pub f: (T) -> string
}

fn widen_mut_field(s: Slot<Dog>): string {
    wide: Slot<dyn> = s
    return "unreachable"
}

fn widen_method_param(m: Matcher<Dog>): string {
    wide: Matcher<dyn> = m
    return "unreachable"
}

fn widen_struct_method_param(p: Pair<Dog>): string {
    wide: Pair<dyn> = p
    return "unreachable"
}

fn widen_fn_field(c: Callback<Dog>): string {
    wide: Callback<dyn> = c
    return "unreachable"
}

fn widen_to_kind(s: Slot<Dog>): string {
    wide: Slot<Struct> = s
    return "unreachable"
}
